import AppKit

/// Clip ▸ Nest (⌘G) and Un-nest (⇧⌘G) — the two grouping commands
/// `EditorViewController.swift` forwards here (spec §4.4, §6).
///
/// Both are one op and one undo step, and every decision that could be the
/// core's is: which track the nest clip lands on, what span it covers, what
/// the nest's canvas is frozen at, where the contents come back to, and which
/// of the documented refusals applies. What is left on this side is the one
/// thing the core cannot answer — what should be SELECTED afterwards — and
/// the menu validation.
///
/// **Selection is read back, never predicted.** A nest replaces n clips with
/// one and an un-nest does the reverse, so in both cases the selection worth
/// having is "whatever is on the timeline now that was not there before": the
/// id-set difference `EditorViewController+AddClips.swift` and
/// `+Clipboard.swift` already use. Reading `next_id` first would work today
/// and would be a third place to be wrong the day the core mints ids
/// differently.
///
/// ## The way in is next door
///
/// The gesture this file looks like it should carry — double-click a nest
/// clip, the timeline and canvas show its contents, a breadcrumb leads back
/// out — is `EditorViewController+NestScope.swift`'s, and it exists (road map
/// 2's phase 9). It is worth reading what USED to be written here, because it
/// was true and it is what changed:
///
/// - **No op could address a clip inside a nest.** Every clip argument in the
///   op table resolves through `Project::clip`, which searches `self.clips`
///   and nothing else, and a nest's clips live in `Nest::clips`. So an inside
///   view could only ever have been READ-ONLY, which is worse than absent:
///   `TimelineView` builds its ops out of the snapshot it is handed, so every
///   drag, trim, split and delete would have emitted an op naming an id the
///   core refuses.
/// - What the feature needed first was a way to NAME a nested clip, and this
///   file said so: "an op-table decision (a path-valued `clip` argument, or a
///   `nest` scope on the envelope), not a new idea". It is the second of
///   those. `core/src/scope.rs` gives the ENVELOPE a `scope`, and the op is
///   applied to a view project whose top level is the nest — so
///   `Project::clip` kept its one meaning, no op gained a branch, and
///   `rc_project_scoped` hands the same view to the app to draw from.
///
/// The round trip — ⇧⌘G, edit, ⌘G — still works and is still the identity on
/// clip content. What it costs, and what going inside now saves, is the nest
/// CLIP's own transform, opacity, fades and volume: the group-level
/// properties, which an unnest throws away and a scope leaves alone.
///
/// Both commands here work INSIDE a nest, with no code of their own for it:
/// they read `document.editedProject` and their ops are stamped with the
/// scope by `ProjectDocument.applyEdit`, so ⌘G in a nest makes a nest in a
/// nest — a legal extra level, bounded by `MAX_NEST_DEPTH` on the merged
/// parent like any other.
extension EditorViewController {

    // MARK: - Nest

    /// ⌘G. Collapses the selection into one clip standing for the group, and
    /// selects it so it can be moved, faded or transformed straight away.
    ///
    /// No refusal beep of its own: `ProjectDocument.applyEdit` already beeps
    /// for both of the core's "nothing changed" answers — an identity (an
    /// empty selection, a lone nest clip) and the overlap rule's refusal of a
    /// nest clip that will not fit — and a second one here would double it. The only beep left is the guard below, which the menu
    /// validation makes unreachable from the menu but not from the key
    /// equivalent's own path.
    func nestSelection() {
        guard let document = document else { return }
        // `selectedClips` is ordered by start, so two runs over the same
        // selection build the same op JSON. `document.selection` is a Set and
        // would not.
        let clips = selectedClips.map { $0.id }
        guard !clips.isEmpty else {
            NSSound.beep()
            return
        }
        let before = document.editedProject.snapshot
        document.applyEdit("Nest", .nest(clips: clips))
        selectClips(addedSince: before, in: document)
    }

    // MARK: - Un-nest

    /// ⇧⌘G. Opens every nest clip in the selection and selects everything
    /// that came out of them.
    ///
    /// All of them in ONE undo step: "open this group" is one intention
    /// whether the user picked one nest or four, and four steps to undo it
    /// would be four surprises. Building every op from the same before-state
    /// is safe because `unnest` only ever removes the clip it names and mints
    /// fresh ids for the rest — no id in the list can be invalidated by an
    /// earlier op in it, and the tracks an earlier op inserts shift indices,
    /// never ids.
    func unnestSelection() {
        guard let document = document else { return }
        let nests = selectedClips.filter { $0.source.nestID != nil }
        guard !nests.isEmpty else {
            NSSound.beep()
            return
        }
        let before = document.editedProject.snapshot
        document.applyEdit(
            nests.count == 1 ? "Un-nest" : "Un-nest Clips",
            nests.map { ReelOp.unnest(clip: $0.id) })
        selectClips(addedSince: before, in: document)
    }

    // MARK: - Validation

    /// The half of `validateUserInterfaceItem` this file owns. nil means "not
    /// mine" and leaves the base file's answer alone.
    ///
    /// Only the CHEAP halves of the core's refusals are mirrored. Menu
    /// validation runs on every event pass, and the third refusal — the nest
    /// clip would overlap something that was NOT selected — can only be
    /// answered by applying the op to a throwaway project, which is what
    /// `EditorViewController+AddClips.swift` does once per invocation and
    /// nothing should do once per keystroke. A live item that beeps is the
    /// right trade; a menu that stutters is not.
    func validateNestAction(_ action: Selector) -> Bool? {
        switch action {
        case #selector(nestClips(_:)):
            let clips = selectedClips
            // The core refuses a selection of one clip that is already a
            // nest: wrapping a group in a group changes nothing anyone can
            // see. Two of them, or a nest and something else, is a real edit.
            if clips.count == 1, clips[0].source.nestID != nil { return false }
            return !clips.isEmpty
        case #selector(unnestClips(_:)):
            return selectedClips.contains { $0.source.nestID != nil }
        default:
            return nil
        }
    }
}
//!
//! The other nests are the parent's own, whole. They have to be: a nest
//! clip INSIDE the scope shows one of them, and `check_clips` would refuse
//! it otherwise.
//!
//! ### The merge
//!
//! [`Project::merge_scope`] writes the nest's `tracks` and `clips`,
//! `next_id`, `media` and `nests` back into a clone of the parent, and
//! leaves the canvas, the markers and the timeline's own clips exactly as
//! they were.
//!
//! **What the merge does NOT write is the half that keeps the markers
//! safe**, and the mutation run said so: giving the view the parent's
//! markers is an EQUIVALENT mutant today, because the three marker ops are
//! refused and the merge discards whatever the view did to them. The empty
//! `markers` on the view is defence — an `insert_time` inside a nest should
//! not spend its time shifting a list that is about to be thrown away — and
//! the load-bearing rule is that a scoped edit never writes markers back. A
//! future change that added them to this list would be the real bug, and it
//! is killed by `a_scoped_edit_does_not_move_the_timelines_markers`.
//!
//! `media` is on that list because of one op. `paste` appends a row for
//! every payload path the project does not already carry
//! (`edit_paste.rs`), so a paste inside a scope that dropped the view's
//! media would leave the pasted clips naming a media the parent has not
//! got, and the merged parent would fail `check_clips` with `clip N refers
//! to unknown media M`. It is a straight replace rather than a union
//! because on this path the list can only GROW: `add_media`, `relink_media`
//! and `remove_media` are refused inside a scope, so nothing else reorders,
//! rewrites or removes a row.
//!
//! `nests` is the subtle one, and the rule is **union first, orphans
//! after**. `unnest` ends by dropping the nest it opened when
//! `nest_is_shown` says nothing else shows it, and `nest_is_shown` asks
//! `every_clip()` — the project's own clips plus every nest's. In a view
//! the project's own clips are the SCOPED nest's, and the parent's timeline
//! clips are not there at all, so un-nesting an inner nest clip that a
//! timeline clip also shows judges that nest unused and drops it; the merge
//! would then leave the parent's clip dangling. Two clips sharing one nest
//! is a real state — `duplicate` clones the source rather than the group —
//! so this is not a corner. The merge therefore takes the UNION, dropping
//! nothing the view dropped, and only then asks the MERGED parent's own
//! `nest_is_shown` about each one. Keeping an orphan is harmless (an
//! orphaned nest is a legal project — `check_nesting` walks them
//! deliberately); dropping a shown one is a corrupt document.
//!
//! ### The result, which has a wider third arm
//!
//! A scoped op answers spec §5.5's three ways like any other, and it exits
//! through the same [`Project::finish`] — but the `Err` arm can fire where
//! no unscoped op can reach, because **two invariants are invisible from
//! inside a scope**:
//!
//! - **The caps.** `format::check_caps` sums the timeline's clips and
//!   tracks with every nest's, deliberately, so 64 nests of 64 tracks
//!   cannot slip past a cap that exists to bound exactly that. A view holds
//!   the nest's clips at top level and the parent's timeline clips nowhere,
//!   so its totals are strictly LOWER than the document's and an op refused
//!   at the parent's cap would be accepted inside a scope.
//! - **The depth.** `check_nesting` starts every walk at level 1, so a view
//!   cannot see how deep the nest it is already sits. A `nest` inside a
//!   scope at depth 8 builds a level-9 stack the view validates happily.
//!
//! Neither can be answered by the view, and neither should be answered
//! twice, so both are answered ONCE, at the end, by validating the MERGED
//! parent. The contract, stated so a caller can rely on it:
//!
//! - `Ok(Outcome::Applied(project))` — the view changed and the merged
//!   parent holds every invariant.
//! - `Ok(Outcome::Identity)` — the op is valid and the view changed
        // A range kept as a whole project is the DOCUMENT's timeline, edited
        // only on a derived document (spec §11 CJ-50); a nest has none.
        Op::KeepRange { .. } => Some("keep_range"),

        // A track's dynamics run on the TIMELINE's per-track sub-mixes (spec
        // §11 AU-150): a nest's whole mix passes through the track holding
        // its nest clip, which is the bus, and a nest's own tracks have no
        // sub-mix of their own to compress. The rest of `set_track` means
        // inside a nest what it means outside (below).
        Op::SetTrack {
            compressor,
            limiter,
            ..
        } if compressor.is_some() || limiter.is_some() => Some("a track's compressor or limiter"),

        // ---- everything else ------------------------------------------
        // Placement, trimming, properties, actions, tracks and grouping all
        // mean in a nest exactly what they mean on the timeline, `nest` and
        // `unnest` included: nesting the whole of a scope mints a FRESH id
        // and puts the new nest clip INSIDE the scoped nest, so no clip
        // ever points at its own container. It is a legal extra level,
