8:Open a [nest](/editing/nests/) to change the clips inside it without taking the group apart. The nest clip keeps its own position, scale, opacity, fades and volume, which [Un-nest](/editing/nests/) would throw away.
21:**Clip ▸ Open Nest** needs exactly one nest clip selected, so it's greyed out with two selected. A double-click doesn't mind what else is selected, because it names its own clip.
25:Because a double-click opens a nest clip, it can't rename one. Use **Clip ▸ Rename…** (<kbd>⇧⌘R</kbd>) or the name field in the Clip tab.
38:- A level is named after the nest clip you went in through, so renaming that clip changes the path.
39:- A long name is shortened in the middle, so `Lower Third 1` and `Lower Third 2` still look different.
69:Every clip edit works inside a nest, in the nest's own times. That includes **Clip ▸ Nest**, which makes a nest inside this one, and **Clip ▸ Un-nest**.
100:| **File ▸ Rename Graphic…** | Off. See [Rename, delete and restore graphics](/graphics/manage-graphics/). |
107:A nest's contents count once, however many clips show it. Un-nest the last clip showing a nest to take its tracks out of the count. Deleting that clip instead leaves the nest's clips and tracks counted.
